            'one view.</figcaption></figure>\n'
            '</section>\n'
            '\n'
            '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
            '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">Operating system support</h2>\n'
            '  <p>This is often the deciding factor. cPanel runs on Linux only. Plesk runs on Linux and on Windows '
            'Server, so if you need IIS, ASP.NET or MSSQL hosting, Plesk is the practical choice. For Linux-only '
            'stacks both work, so the decision moves to the points below.</p>\n'
            '</section>\n'
            '\n'
            '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
            '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">Interface and workflow</h2>\n'
            '  <p>cPanel splits the work in two: end users log in to cPanel to manage their own site, while '
            'administrators and resellers use WHM to create accounts, set packages and manage the server. Plesk puts '
            'both roles in one interface and shows each user only what their role allows.</p>\n'
            '  <p>cPanel is the more familiar panel among customers who have used shared hosting before, which reduces '
            "support questions when you migrate them. Plesk's single interface is popular with agencies that manage "
            'many sites for their own clients.</p>\n'
            '</section>\n'
            '\n'
            '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
            '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">Ecosystem and integrations</h2>\n'
            '  <p>cPanel has the larger hosting-industry ecosystem. Billing automation such as <a '
            'href="/whmcs-license" class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">WHMCS</a>, app installers such as '
            '<a href="/softaculous-license" class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">Softaculous</a>, <a '
            'href="/cloudlinux-license" class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">CloudLinux</a>, <a '
            'href="/imunify360-license" class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">Imunify360</a> and <a '
            'href="/jetbackup-license" class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">JetBackup</a> all integrate '
            'with it, and <a href="/litespeed-license" class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">LiteSpeed</a> '
            'offers a WHM plugin.</p>\n'
            '  <p>Plesk offers extensions for WordPress management (WordPress Toolkit), Git and Docker, which suit '
            'developers and agencies. Many of the same security and backup products also support Plesk.</p>\n'
            '</section>\n'
            '\n'
            '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
            '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">WordPress hosting</h2>\n'
            "  <p>Plesk's WordPress Toolkit is a strong point for managing many WordPress sites from one place: "
            'updates, cloning, staging and security checks. On the cPanel side, <a href="/wp-squared-license" '
            'class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">WP Squared</a> is cPanel\'s WordPress-focused platform '
            'for dedicated WordPress hosting. If WordPress is most of your workload, compare these two directly.</p>\n'
            '</section>\n'
            '\n'
            '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
            '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">Pricing and licensing</h2>\n'
            '  <p>cPanel licenses are tiered by the number of accounts on a server, so the cost rises as you host more '
            'customers. Plesk uses editions with domain limits and feature differences. The retail price from the '
            'vendor is rarely what a hosting business has to pay: buying through a license provider such as LicenBase '
            'usually costs less for the same genuine license. See the current <a href="/cpanel-license" '
            'class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">cPanel</a> and <a href="/plesk-license" '
            'class="font-semibold text-brand hover:underline">Plesk</a> pricing for VPS and dedicated servers.</p>\n'
            '</section>\n'
            '\n'
            '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
            '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">Which one should you choose?</h2>\n'
            '  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">\n'
            '    <li><strong>Choose cPanel</strong> if you run Linux, plan to sell hosting or resell accounts, and '
            'want the widest set of billing, security and backup integrations.</li>\n'
            '    <li><strong>Choose Plesk</strong> if you need Windows hosting, or you manage many WordPress or '
            'developer sites for agency clients.</li>\n'
            '    <li><strong>Moving between them</strong> is possible. Both vendors provide migration tools, so the '
            'choice is less permanent than it looks, but plan for a maintenance window.</li>\n'
            '  </ul>\n'
            '</section>\n'
            '\n'
            '<section class="space-y-4">\n'
            '  <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-navy">Key takeaways</h2>\n'
            '  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">\n'
            '    <li>Need Windows? Plesk. Linux-only and resale-focused? cPanel is the usual pick.</li>\n'
            '    <li>cPanel pricing follows account count; Plesk pricing follows edition.</li>\n'
            '    <li>Check which add-ons you need (billing, security, backups) and confirm they support your panel '
            'before you buy.</li>\n'
            '  </ul>\n'
            '</section>\n',
